  "strategy": "<section title=\"Approach and Rationale\">\n        We employ a hybrid optimization strategy combining a multi-resolution analysis with geometric transformation to explore the space of possible function configurations. The central idea is to transform the function samples using a spherical-like representation that preserves the integral constraint, followed by gradient-based optimization over these transformed coordinates. This differs from traditional local search or stochastic methods by leveraging dimensionality-reducing mappings and induced symmetry properties. The key innovation is representing the function at varying resolutions while maintaining the integral constraint through a projective neural network. We escape local optima by measuring gradient alignment across resolutions.\n    </section>\n\n    <section title=\"Mathematical Specification\">\n        <subsection title=\"Representation\">\n            Represent function as spherical harmonic coefficients (Y_l^m) for increasing resolutions. Energy is mapped from discrete samples to harmonic coefficients:\n            E_optimizer = \u2211_{l=0}^{L} ( |Y_l|\u00b7sqrt(4\u03c0/(2l+1)) )\u00b2\n            Subject to: M(h) = (1/(n_points/2))\u00b7\u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1\n        </subsection>\n\n        <subsection title=\"Objective\">\n            The cost function is defined as:\n            C_smooth = -min(log\u211d \u03c1_{max}(k) - \u03bb\u00b7\u2211_l(1-l^2)\u00b7|c_l|^2\n            Where \u03c1_{max}(k) is the average log-integral overlap computed along all possible lags k.\n\n        <subsection title=\"Constraints\">\n            0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 holds by projection. Integral constraint is encoded through:\n            C_L2(h) = \u2211h[i]\u00b7dx = 1 (numerically enforced)\n            \n            Projective normalization: h(x) = clip(sin(\u03b8(x)), 0, 1) where \u03b8(x) comes from spherical embedding.\n        </subsection>\n    </section>\n\n    <section title=\"Implementation Procedure\">\n        <subsection title=\"Core Algorithm\">\n            <ol>\n                <li>Phase 0: Initialization</li>\n                <ul>\n                    <li>Start from initial_h_values if provided (size n_initial)</li>\n                    <li>Normalize samples to satisfy position constraints</li>\n                    <li>Transform to spherical harmonic coefficients C_l (resolution-dependent)</li>\n                </ul>\n                <li>Phase 1: Projection Training</li>\n                <ul>\n                    <li>Train a simple MLP to map harmonic coefficients to function samples</li>\n                    <li>Evaluate path cost C_light at each iteration</li>\n                    <li>Backpropagate through normalized spherical coordinates</li>\n                </ul>\n                <li>Phase 2: Resolution Doubling</li>\n                <ul>\n                    <li>Interpolate and orthogonally transform to higher resolution</li>\n                    <li>Filter coefficients to maintain unit energy</li>\n                    <li>Continue optimization with projection steps</li>\n                </ul>\n                <li>Phase 3: Progressive Refinement</li>\n                <ul>\n                    <li>Apply iterative Riemannian gradient steps via spherical coordinates</li>\n                    <li>Adjust logarithmically spaced training examples for all lags</li>\n                    <li>Validate against control system test (overlap curve consistency)</li>\n                </ul>\n            </ol>\n        </subsection>\n\n        <subsection title=\"Objective Calculation\">\n            <code>\n                def compute_cost(h):\n                    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n                    max_corr = np.max(corr)\n                    return float(max_corr * (2.0 / len(h)))\n            </code>\n        </subsection>\n\n        <subsection title=\"Gradient Handling\">\n            <p>Use automatic differentiation through the harmonic transform, or explicit computations with:</p>\n            <code>\n                def compute_gradients(h):\n                    n = len(h)\n                    # Compute correlation components\n                    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n                    max_val = np.max(corr)\n                    # Create smooth max penalty\n                    smooth = alpha * (corr/max_val - 1)\n                    exp_v = np.exp(smooth)\n                    weights = exp_v / np.sum(exp_v)\n                    # Gradient code follows provided parent context\n            </code>\n        </subsection>\n    </section>\n\n    <section title=\"Search Choices and Variations\">\n        <subsection title=\"Resolution Progression\">\n            <p>Start at n_init (If parent provided: 480), double resolution every 2000 evaluations until either:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Architectural singularity (trivial function)</li>\n                <li>Time constraint (500\u00d7 total operations)</li>\n            </ul>\n        </subsection>\n\n        <subsection title=\"Harmonic Basis Selection\">\n            <p>Select 8-16 low-frequency harmonics for base resolution. At higher resolution:</p>\n            <code>\n                l_max = min(floor(sqrt(n)), 250)\n                bands = [(i+1)//2 for i in range(min(16, l_max//2))]\n            </code>\n        </subsection>\n\n        <subsection title=\"Gradient Descent Parameters\">\n            <p>Start schedule:</p>\n            <code>\n                learning_rate = 0.01\n                momentum = 0.9\n                decay = adaptive(alpha=0.99, min_step=0.0001)\n            </code>\n        </subsection>\n    </section>\n\n    <section title=\"Computation Budget and Robustness\">\n        <ul>\n            <li>Doubling time: O(2^k) operations at resolution k</li>\n            <li>Memory usage: O(n_points) samples + O(res^2) coefficients</li>\n            <li>CPU detection: switch to coarse grain multiprocessing when n>500</li>\n            <li>Termination triggers:</li>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Improvement stall (500\u00d7 steps): fallback to coarser initial</li>\n                <li>No significant improvement over rounded 0.38085 bound</li>\n                <li>Double-time consumed \u2265 time budget \u2192 scale down learning rate</li>\n            </ul>\n        </ul>\n    </section>\n\n    <section title=\"Validation and Return Contract\">\n        <ol>\n            <li>Final function must satisfy: \u2200i, 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1</li>\n            <li>Integral check: sum(h) * dx \u2248 n/2 with |error| \u2264 1e-4</li>\n            <li objective comparison: Compute C_old and C_new for same resolution</li>\n            <li>CVerifier( h ): Compute max correlation with 10\u00d7 random lag samples</li>\n            <li>Output format: (h, C_value, n_points)</li>\n        </ol>\n    </section>",
